1027 #edb/ref 17592186048132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050890 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050890 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050890 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050890 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050890 #edb/ref 1031 "The Square Root of a Perfect Square With Domain Restrictions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050890 #edb/ref 1033 3729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050890 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6eaa79dd-c90f-5e62-94cb-871b5a23b05e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178515 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186178516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178515 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186178517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178515 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186178518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178515 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186178519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178515 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186178520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178515 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178515 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178515 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9d10b6a6-f98d-52cb-975a-842633722f73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178516 #edb/ref 1080 "a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178516 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178516 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c0cfe5f9-b62f-5b51-ab1d-bce042907edc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178517 #edb/ref 1080 "a^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178517 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178517 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1a73cf8e-00c3-5c0e-a4a4-3dd14653ca57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178518 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178518 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178518 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "414b8ab5-e58e-5e5f-afd8-5d24e4765f53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178519 #edb/ref 1080 "a^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178519 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178519 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "50d7d27e-a8b8-55fb-920e-3989c1684b8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178520 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178520 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186178520 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5af6a186-f70b-53bc-a5e9-a363d3f26134" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179461 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1352" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179461 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{y^4 z^{6}}$ given that $z \\lt 0$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179461 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "27032e03-f6b6-5f66-b514-d3b9c4f79ec0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179461 #edb/ref 1074 "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can simplify the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{y^4 z^{6}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt{y^2 z^3 \\cdot y^2 z^3} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt{\\left(y^2 z^3\\right)^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\begin{vmatrix}y^2 z^3 & \\\\[5pt] &= & y^2 & \\cdot & z^3 & \\\\[5pt] &= & y^2 & \\cdot\\left.\\end{vmatrix}z\\right|^3 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\mid y^2 \\mid = y^2\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that $z \\lt 0$, and to take the absolute value of any negative number we need to flip the sign, so we have\n\n$$\n\\mid z \\mid = -z\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}y^{2} & \\cdot\\left.\\end{vmatrix}z\\right|^{3} = y^{2} \\cdot (-z)^{3} =-y^{2}z^{3}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179462 #edb/ref 1069 "e-1353" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179462 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{r^2}{s^2}}$ given that $s>0$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179462 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4b7c6339-6e9c-5047-a95f-3daf41db4a8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179462 #edb/ref 1074 "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can simplify the given expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{r^2}{s^2}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{r}{s} \\cdot \\dfrac{r}{s}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{r}{s}\\right)^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\left|\\dfrac{r}{s}\\right| \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{\\begin{vmatrix}r &}{\\mid s\\end{vmatrix}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $s \\gt 0$, and we know that the absolute value of any positive number is just itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\mid s \\mid = s\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\begin{vmatrix}r &}{\\begin{vmatrix}s &} = \\dfrac{\\end{vmatrix}r\\end{vmatrix}}{s}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179761 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186179915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179761 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179761 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying an Expression Given That Some Variables Are Negative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179761 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179761 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a7d2284f-c4ba-571f-91a6-be472f185c0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179762 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179762 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179762 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179762 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179762 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying an Expression Given That Some Variables Are Non-Negative" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179762 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179762 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5b8f0a91-f0de-5907-9c96-eac8a3a0e7cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179879 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179879 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179879 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179879 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179879 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179879 #edb/ref 1045 "The Square Root of a Perfect Square With Domain Restrictions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179879 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179879 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179879 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ae806ffe-8add-5f7b-a38d-5d4077cc72cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180161 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180161 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2930" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180161 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\sqrt{a^{2}}$ given that $a > 0$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180161 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec502873-fbf5-5e68-afb5-0d5690849cc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180517 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180517 #edb/ref 1061 36113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180517 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c8a28231-be54-56e8-b63b-3e1a42d1360e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180517 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180521 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180521 #edb/ref 1061 36179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180521 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8e668d2a-9da1-5eaf-be19-c24fd0a3b12a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180521 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180547 #edb/ref 1061 4285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180547 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9a309239-eefc-54be-8199-bbf772d5350e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180547 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180548 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180548 #edb/ref 1061 4287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180548 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "35d16b06-b444-5257-a405-5223a9834174" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180548 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180733 #edb/ref 1064 "The square root of a squared quantity can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2} = \\mid x \\mid\n$$\n\nHowever, if the domain of the variable is restricted, some expressions might allow further simplification.\n\nFor example, suppose we know that $a>0$. Let's use this information to fully simplify $\\sqrt{36a^2}$.\n\nWe can start by simplifying the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{36a^{2}} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{(6a)^{2}} &=  \\\\\n\\mid 6a \\mid &=  \\\\\n&= \\begin{vmatrix}6 & \\cdot & a\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6 \\mid a \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $a>0$, and we know that the absolute value of any positive number is just itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid a &= a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n6 \\mid a \\mid = 6a\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180733 #edb/ref 1065 5905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180733 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "414812ea-abcc-59df-98fa-8a79ae36a1a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180733 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180736 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's see what changes if we consider square roots of perfect squares when some variables are negative.\n\nFor example, suppose that $t \\lt 0$. Let's use this to simplify the expression $\\sqrt{t^2}$.\n\nSimplifying this expression in the usual way, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sqrt{t^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt{(t)^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\mid t \\mid.& \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $t \\lt 0$. To take the absolute value of a negative number, we need to flip the sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\mid t \\mid = -t\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{t^2}} = {\\color{red}-t}\n$$\n\nYou can convince yourself that this is correct by plugging a few values of $t$ into the above equation. Provided that $t$ is negative, this expression will always give a true statement.\n\nFor example, suppose that $t = -2$. Substituting into the left-hand side of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{t^{2}} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}} &=  \\\\\n\\sqrt{4} &=  \\\\\n&= 2 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=-2$ into the right-hand side of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-t &=  \\\\\n- (-2) &=  \\\\\n&= 2 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sides give the same result, the statement is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180736 #edb/ref 1065 5928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180736 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1f0207ef-9d6a-584b-8d44-a92c732cce4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180736 #edb/ref 1067 "The Square Root of a Perfect Square With Negative Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
